\title{Universal Properties of Humor:\\ Colimits, Submodularity, and the Uniqueness of Comic Surprise}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous order- and category-theoretic model of comic surprise. A \emph{setup} is modelled as a nonempty finite configuration $S \subset \mathbb{R}$ of interpretive readings, ordered by refinement, and its \emph{surprise} by the range $H(S) = \max S - \min S$. We prove five groups of results. First, three axioms --- position blindness, staged telling, and monotonicity in the reading gap --- determine the invariant completely: every \emph{humor scale} is $V(m,M) = c\,(M-m)$ with $c = V(0,1) \ge 0$; the proof rests on a from-scratch monotone Cauchy theorem. Second, surprise is a \emph{submodular valuation}: $H(S \cup T) + H(S \cap T) \le H(S) + H(T)$ whenever $S \cap T \neq \varnothing$, strictly strengthening subadditivity. Third, humor is literally a colimit: the category of setups has all binary coproducts (the joint setup) but not all binary products (disjoint setups have none), so the punchline always exists while the expected resolution may not. Fourth, a terminal object maximises every functor into $\mathbb{R}$, so universal jokes maximise surprise and all universal jokes over a fixed setup are equally surprising; the converse is \emph{false}, and is repaired exactly by localising at the hull, after which maximal humor becomes equivalent to hull-universality. Fifth, surprise equals the metric diameter, is $2$-Lipschitz for the Hausdorff distance with a sharp paraphrase bound, and its correlation with funniness ratings is not a theorem but holds exactly under monovariance, by Chebyshev's sum inequality.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} submodular valuation, colimit, terminal object, thin category, Hausdorff distance, Cauchy functional equation, Chebyshev sum inequality, incongruity theory.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The incongruity slogan and its formalisation}

The dominant account of verbal humor is the \emph{incongruity--resolution} account: a setup induces an expectation, the punchline violates it, and the listener resolves the violation within a reinterpreted frame. Attractive as it is, the account is not quantitative. It says nothing about how the surprise of a compound joke relates to the surprise of its parts, whether the quantity is stable under rewording, or whether ``the expected resolution'' is guaranteed to exist at all.

The programme pursued here formalises the slogan
\begin{center}\emph{the punchline is a colimit; the expected resolution is a limit,}\end{center}
and pushes it until it either becomes a theorem or breaks. Both happen. The colimit half is unconditionally true. The limit half is \emph{conditionally false} --- limits may not exist --- and this failure is the precise technical content of the slogan. A companion conjecture, that maximal humor characterises universality, is half-true and is repaired by an explicit localisation.

\subsection{The model}

We model interpretive readings as points of $\mathbb{R}$: an \emph{interpretive line} on which the literal reading and the various figurative readings each occupy a position, distance measuring how far apart their frames are. Nothing depends essentially on one dimension --- Section~\ref{sec:metric} lifts the invariant to an arbitrary metric space --- but the one-dimensional case is where the combinatorics is sharpest.

\begin{definition}[Setup]
A \textbf{setup} is a nonempty finite subset $S \subset \mathbb{R}$. Setups are ordered by \textbf{refinement}, $S \le T \iff S \subseteq T$: the setup $T$ is the same joke told so that strictly more readings are audible.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The category of setups]
$\Setup$ is the category whose objects are setups, with a unique morphism $S \to T$ whenever $S \le T$. It is \emph{thin} (at most one morphism between any two objects), so all coherence conditions hold automatically and every construction is determined by its order-theoretic content.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Surprise]\label{def:surprise}
The \textbf{surprise} (or humor) of a setup $S$ is $H(S) = \max S - \min S$.
\end{definition}

Puns correspond to setups of small range; absurdist jokes to setups of large range. The order-theoretic content of $\Setup$ is that it is the lattice of finite subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ restricted to nonempty ones. That restriction is not cosmetic: it is exactly what makes products fail (Theorem~\ref{thm:noprod}).

\section{The invariant is forced}

The obvious objection to Definition~\ref{def:surprise} is that the formula was \emph{chosen}. This section removes the choice.

\begin{definition}[Humor scale]
A \textbf{humor scale} is a function $V : \mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$, whose value $V(m,M)$ is the surprise of a setup with extreme readings $m \le M$, satisfying:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(A1)] \textbf{Position blindness}: $V(m+c, M+c) = V(m,M)$ for all $m \le M$ and $c \in \mathbb{R}$;
\item[(A2)] \textbf{Staged telling}: $V(a,b) + V(b,c) = V(a,c)$ for all $a \le b \le c$;
\item[(A3)] \textbf{Monotonicity}: $V(a,b) \le V(a,c)$ for all $a \le b \le c$.
\end{itemize}
Its \textbf{unit} is $c_V := V(0,1)$.
\end{definition}

Each axiom has a comic reading. (A1): comedy is about relative displacement of frames, not absolute location. (A2): a joke told in two stages accumulates the surprise of the stages. (A3): a wider divergence of readings is never less surprising.

\begin{lemma}[Nonnegativity of the unit]
$c_V \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(A2) at $a=b=c=0$ gives $V(0,0)+V(0,0)=V(0,0)$, so $V(0,0)=0$; (A3) gives $V(0,0) \le V(0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone Cauchy]\label{thm:cauchy}
Let $g : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $g(s+t) = g(s)+g(t)$ for all $s,t \ge 0$ and $g(s) \le g(t)$ whenever $0 \le s \le t$. Then $g(t) = g(1)\,t$ for all $t \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Additivity at $s=t=0$ gives $g(0)=0$. Induction gives $g(ks)=k\,g(s)$ for $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $s \ge 0$; at $s=1/n$ this yields $g(1/n)=g(1)/n$ and hence $g(k/n)=g(1)k/n$ for all nonnegative rationals. For arbitrary $t \ge 0$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}$ set $k=\lfloor nt \rfloor$, so $k/n \le t \le (k+1)/n$; monotonicity sandwiches $g(t)$ between $g(1)k/n$ and $g(1)(k+1)/n$, and $g(1)t$ lies in the same interval, whence $|g(t)-g(1)t| \le g(1)/n$. Archimedean property finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of surprise]\label{thm:uniq}
Every humor scale satisfies $V(m,M) = c_V\,(M-m)$ for all $m \le M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $g(t)=V(0,t)$. For $s,t \ge 0$, (A2) gives $V(0,s)+V(s,s+t)=V(0,s+t)$ and (A1) gives $V(s,t+s)=V(0,t)$, so $g(s+t)=g(s)+g(t)$; (A3) makes $g$ monotone. Theorem~\ref{thm:cauchy} gives $g(t)=c_V t$, and (A1) shifts $V(m,M)$ to $g(M-m)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One degree of freedom]
If $V,W$ are humor scales with $c_V = c_W$ then $V = W$ on $\{(m,M): m \le M\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Consistency]
The \emph{range scale} $V(m,M)=M-m$ satisfies (A1)--(A3) and has unit $1$; Definition~\ref{def:surprise} is its normalisation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Transfer]\label{cor:transfer}
On a setup $S$, every humor scale satisfies $V(\min S, \max S) = c_V \cdot H(S)$. Consequently every result below about $H$ holds for every humor scale with all bounds scaled by $c_V$; in particular submodularity (Theorem~\ref{thm:submod}) and the Hausdorff bound (Theorem~\ref{thm:lip}).
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Monotonicity is essential]
Without (A3), a Hamel-basis solution of the Cauchy equation gives a position-blind, stage-additive functional that is wildly discontinuous and not proportional to the range. The order axiom is what makes this a \emph{measurement} theory.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact combination law}

Throughout, $S \cup T$ is the \textbf{joint} setup (both jokes told at once) and $S \cap T$ the \textbf{shared} setup. We assume $S \cap T \neq \varnothing$ so the shared setup is itself a setup.

\begin{theorem}[Submodularity]\label{thm:submod}
For setups $S,T$ with $S \cap T \neq \varnothing$,
\[ H(S\cup T) + H(S\cap T) \;\le\; H(S) + H(T). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $S \cap T \subseteq S$ and $S \cap T \subseteq T$,
\[ \min S \le \min(S\cap T) \le \max(S\cap T) \le \max S, \qquad \min T \le \min(S\cap T) \le \max(S\cap T) \le \max T. \]
Also $\max(S\cup T) = \max(\max S,\max T)$ and $\min(S\cup T)=\min(\min S,\min T)$. Using $\max(A,B)+\min(A,B)=A+B$ and splitting into the four cases determined by which of $S,T$ realises the joint extremes, each case is a linear combination of the displayed inequalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Nonnegativity]\label{prop:nonneg}
$H(S) \ge 0$ for every setup $S$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Subadditivity]
For setups sharing a reading, $H(S\cup T) \le H(S)+H(T)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:submod} together with Proposition~\ref{prop:nonneg}.
\end{proof}

Subadditivity is the version one might guess; submodularity is strictly stronger and quantifies the defect. Comic material lying in the overlap is counted \emph{once}: the shortfall of the joint joke below the sum of its pieces is at least $H(S\cap T)$.

\begin{proposition}[Colimit dominates limit]\label{prop:interunion}
For setups with $S \cap T \neq \varnothing$, $H(S\cap T) \le H(S\cup T)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$H(S\cap T) \le H(S) \le H(S\cup T)$ by monotonicity of $H$ under refinement.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Submodularity is the right shape]
Submodular set functions are the discrete analogue of concave functions and carry a diminishing-returns reading: adding a reading to a larger setup gains no more than adding it to a smaller one. Comic economy is thereby identified with a well-studied combinatorial structure, opening the theory to greedy-optimisation methods (Section~\ref{sec:algo}).
\end{remark}

\section{Colimits always exist; limits need not}

Recall that a \textbf{binary coproduct} of $X,Y$ is an object $P$ with morphisms $\iota_X : X \to P$, $\iota_Y : Y \to P$ such that every pair $f : X \to Z$, $g : Y \to Z$ factors uniquely through $P$; a \textbf{binary product} is the dual notion. Coproducts are the simplest colimits, products the simplest limits.

\begin{theorem}[The punchline is a colimit]\label{thm:coprod}
The joint setup $S \cup T$, with the inclusion morphisms $S \to S\cup T \leftarrow T$, is the binary coproduct of $S$ and $T$ in $\Setup$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Inclusions exist since $S \subseteq S\cup T \supseteq T$. Given $Z$ with morphisms from $S$ and $T$ --- that is, $S \subseteq Z$ and $T \subseteq Z$ --- we get $S\cup T \subseteq Z$, the mediating morphism. Uniqueness and all coherence conditions are automatic because $\Setup$ is thin.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\Setup$ has all binary coproducts.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The expected resolution may fail to exist]\label{thm:noprod}
If $S \cap T = \varnothing$ then $S$ and $T$ have \emph{no} binary product in $\Setup$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $P$ were a product with projections $\pi_S : P \to S$, $\pi_T : P \to T$. In $\Setup$ these are the inclusions $P \subseteq S$ and $P \subseteq T$, so $P \subseteq S \cap T = \varnothing$. But every setup is nonempty, so $P$ contains some $x$, which would lie in both $S$ and $T$ --- contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Disjointness, not distinctness]
The hypothesis cannot be weakened to $S \ne T$: if $S \cap T \neq \varnothing$, the intersection \emph{is} the product. The dichotomy is exactly between overlapping and non-overlapping frames.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{Interpretation.} The listener's expected resolution is the greatest common reading --- a limit, a consensus. The punchline is the least world in which every reading is simultaneously alive --- a colimit, a fusion. Theorems~\ref{thm:coprod} and~\ref{thm:noprod} say the fusion always exists while the consensus need not. When two interpretive frames share nothing, the joke has no expectation to subvert, yet still has a punchline. This is the exact sense in which \emph{humor is a colimit}.

\section{Universality: a theorem, a refutation, and a repair}

\subsection{Terminal objects maximise every real-valued invariant}

Regard $\mathbb{R}$ as a category with a unique morphism $x \to y$ iff $x \le y$; a functor $F : \mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}$ is then an assignment of reals to objects, monotone along morphisms.

\begin{theorem}[Terminality certifies maximality]\label{thm:term}
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be any category, $F : \mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R}$ any functor and $T$ a terminal object. Then $F(X) \le F(T)$ for every object $X$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Terminality gives a morphism $!_X : X \to T$; functoriality gives $F(!_X) : F(X) \to F(T)$ in $\mathbb{R}$, which \emph{is} the inequality $F(X) \le F(T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dual]
If $I$ is initial then $F(I) \le F(X)$ for every $X$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:term} is one line, but it is the categorical core of the universality conjecture, and it holds with total generality.

\subsection{The category of jokes over a setup}

\begin{definition}
Fix setups $S \le U$, with $U$ an ambient \emph{universe} of admissible readings. The category $\Joke(S,U)$ has as objects the setups $T$ with $S \le T \le U$ and as morphisms the refinements; it is thin.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[The universe is the universal joke]
$U$ is terminal in $\Joke(S,U)$.
\end{proposition}

Since $H$ is monotone under refinement, $H$ restricts to a functor $\Joke(S,U) \to \mathbb{R}$.

\begin{theorem}[Universal jokes are the funniest]\label{thm:univfunny}
If $J^\ast \in \Joke(S,U)$ is terminal then $H(J) \le H(J^\ast)$ for every $J \in \Joke(S,U)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:term} applied to the surprise functor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Well-definedness of universal humor]
Any two terminal objects of $\Joke(S,U)$ have equal surprise.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:univfunny} twice and use antisymmetry.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The converse is false}

\begin{definition}
Let $\mathrm{pun} := \{0,1\}$ and $\mathrm{pun}^{+} := \{0,\tfrac12,1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$H(\mathrm{pun}) = H(\mathrm{pun}^{+}) = 1$ and $\mathrm{pun} \subsetneq \mathrm{pun}^{+}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Surprise does not reflect refinement]
There exist setups $S < T$ with $H(S) = H(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the converse]\label{thm:refute}
There is a category $\Joke(S,U)$ containing a non-terminal object of maximal humor. Take $S=\mathrm{pun}$, $U=\mathrm{pun}^{+}$, $J=\mathrm{pun}$: every $K \in \Joke(S,U)$ satisfies $H(K) \le H(U) = 1 = H(J)$, yet $J$ is not a greatest element, since $U$ does not refine into $J$.
\end{theorem}

Hence \emph{universal $\Rightarrow$ funniest} is a theorem while \emph{funniest $\Rightarrow$ universal} is false.

\begin{remark}[The failure is structural]
$H$ depends only on the two extreme readings, so every refinement adding purely \emph{interior} readings alters the joke without altering the measurement. Maximal surprise is attained on an entire upward-closed family, not at a single object. The counterexample uses a genuinely strict refinement, so it is not an artefact of a degenerate category.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The repair: the hull quotient}

\begin{definition}[Hull]
The \textbf{hull} of a setup is the interpretive interval $\hull(S) := (\min S, \max S) \in \Hull := \{(p,q)\in\mathbb{R}^2 : p \le q\}$, preordered by \emph{inclusion}: $(p,q) \le (p',q')$ iff $p' \le p$ and $q \le q'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\hull$ is monotone, hence a functor $\Setup \to \Hull$, and the length $\lambda(p,q)=q-p$ is a functor $\Hull \to \mathbb{R}$; surprise factors as $H = \lambda\circ\hull$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Exactly what surprise reflects]\label{thm:reflect}
If $S \le T$ then $H(S) = H(T) \iff \hull(S) = \hull(T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is factorisation. ($\Rightarrow$) monotonicity of the hull gives $\min T \le \min S$ and $\max S \le \max T$; if the lengths agree, both inequalities are forced to be equalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Hull-universality]
$J \in \Joke(S,U)$ is \textbf{hull-universal} if $\hull(K) \le \hull(J)$ for every $K \in \Joke(S,U)$: it is terminal in the hull quotient.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Terminality descends]
If $J$ is terminal in $\Joke(S,U)$ then $J$ is hull-universal; hull-universality is a genuine weakening of terminality.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Hull-universal jokes are the funniest]
If $J$ is hull-universal then $H(K) \le H(J)$ for all $K \in \Joke(S,U)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The universality conjecture, repaired]\label{thm:repair}
Let $S \le U$ and $J \in \Joke(S,U)$. Then $J$ has maximal humor in $\Joke(S,U)$ if and only if $J$ is hull-universal.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) is the previous theorem. ($\Rightarrow$) apply maximality to $K=U$; combined with $H(J) \le H(U)$ this forces $H(J)=H(U)$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:reflect} gives $\hull(J)=\hull(U)$. Every $K$ satisfies $K \le U$, hence $\hull(K) \le \hull(U) = \hull(J)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-degeneracy]
The hull functor is not injective: $\mathrm{pun} \neq \mathrm{pun}^{+}$ but $\hull(\mathrm{pun}) = \hull(\mathrm{pun}^{+})$.
\end{theorem}

Together, Theorems~\ref{thm:refute}, \ref{thm:repair} and the last give a complete account: ``funniest = universal'' fails precisely and only because the hull functor is non-injective, and becomes an equivalence after localising at hull-equivalence. The counterexample was a mismatch of resolution between an invariant that sees intervals and a category that sees finite sets.

\section{Metric stability}\label{sec:metric}

\subsection{Surprise is a diameter}

\begin{theorem}
For every nonempty finite $S \subset \mathbb{R}$, $H(S) = \diam(S)$, the diameter of $S$ in the metric space $\mathbb{R}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both extremes lie in $S$, so $\diam S \ge \dist(\min S,\max S) = H(S)$. Conversely every $x,y \in S$ satisfies $|x-y| \le \max S - \min S = H(S)$, so $\diam S \le H(S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Metric surprise]
For a subset $s$ of a pseudometric space, put $H(s) := \diam(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Generalisation]
In an arbitrary pseudometric space of readings, metric surprise is monotone under inclusion of bounded sets and satisfies the shared-context subadditivity law $H(s\cup t) \le H(s)+H(t)$ when $s \cap t \neq \varnothing$.
\end{proposition}

Thus the one-dimensional model is not ad hoc; it is the $\mathbb{R}$-instance of a metric-space invariant, and readings may equally be embedded in a high-dimensional semantic space.

\subsection{Lipschitz stability}

Let $d_H$ denote the Hausdorff distance between bounded nonempty sets.

\begin{theorem}[$2$-Lipschitz]\label{thm:lip}
For bounded nonempty $s,t$ with finite Hausdorff distance,
\[ \bigl|\diam(s) - \diam(t)\bigr| \le 2\,d_H(s,t). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $\delta > d_H(s,t)$ every $x \in s$ has $x' \in t$ with $d(x,x') < \delta$. For $x,y \in s$ choose such $x',y'$; the quadrilateral inequality gives $d(x,y) \le \delta + d(x',y') + \delta \le \diam(t)+2\delta$. Taking suprema, $\diam(s) \le \diam(t)+2\delta$; let $\delta \downarrow d_H(s,t)$ and symmetrise.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Hypotheses are load-bearing]
Finiteness of the Hausdorff distance and boundedness of the comparison set cannot be dropped: under the convention that an infinite extended Hausdorff distance yields $d_H = 0$, the inequality would be violated by sets of arbitrarily different diameters.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The paraphrase bound}

\begin{theorem}[Paraphrase stability]\label{thm:para}
Let $S$ be a setup and $f : \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $|f(x)-x| \le \varepsilon$ for every $x \in S$. Then $|H(f(S)) - H(S)| \le 2\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $m = \min S$, $M = \max S$. Every $y=f(x)$ with $x \in S$ satisfies $y \le x+\varepsilon \le M+\varepsilon$, so $\max f(S) \le M+\varepsilon$; and $f(M) \ge M-\varepsilon$ lies in $f(S)$, so $\max f(S) \ge M-\varepsilon$. Symmetrically $m-\varepsilon \le \min f(S) \le m+\varepsilon$. Subtracting the four bounds gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
The constant $2$ cannot be improved: with $S=\{0,1\}$, $f(x)=3x-1$ and $\varepsilon=1$, each reading moves by exactly $1$, $f(S)=\{-1,2\}$, and $|H(f(S))-H(S)| = 2 = 2\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

By Corollary~\ref{cor:transfer}, every humor scale is $2c_V$-Lipschitz for the Hausdorff distance.

\section{Correlation with funniness: what is, and is not, a theorem}

\begin{definition}[Empirical covariance]
For a finite index set $s$ of jokes and attributes $f,g : s \to \mathbb{R}$,
\[ \Cov_s(f,g) = \frac{\sum_{i\in s} f(i)g(i)}{|s|} - \left(\frac{\sum_{i\in s} f(i)}{|s|}\right)\left(\frac{\sum_{i\in s} g(i)}{|s|}\right). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Monovariance]
$f$ and $g$ \textbf{monovary} on $s$ if there is no pair $i,j \in s$ with $f(i) < f(j)$ and $g(j) < g(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The correlation claim, correctly guarded]\label{thm:corr}
If $f$ and $g$ monovary on $s$ then $\Cov_s(f,g) \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Monovariance implies Chebyshev's sum inequality $\bigl(\sum_i f(i)\bigr)\bigl(\sum_i g(i)\bigr) \le |s|\sum_i f(i)g(i)$; dividing by $|s|^2>0$ rearranges to the claim. The empty sample is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The hypothesis cannot be dropped]
There is a two-joke dataset with strictly negative covariance: humors $(0,1)$ and ratings $(1,0)$ give $\Cov = -\tfrac14$.
\end{theorem}

So ``surprise correlates with funniness'' is \emph{not} a theorem of the categorical theory; it is a property of the data.

\begin{definition}[The hundred-joke suite]
For $i < 100$, let joke $J_i$ have setup $\{0,i\}$, so $H(J_i) = i$ exactly, and let the rating model saturate: $R_i = \min(i,50)$, reflecting the ceiling of finite rating scales.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$H$ and $R$ are both monotone in $i$, hence monovary, hence $\Cov(H,R) \ge 0$ across the hundred-joke suite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[A concrete positive sample]
For the three-joke sample --- a pun ($H=1$, rated $2$), a piece of wordplay ($H=3$, rated $5$) and an absurdist joke ($H=10$, rated $8$) --- the covariance is strictly positive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[Scope]
The suite uses a \emph{synthetic} monotone rating model; it tests internal consistency of the formalism, not human response.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Computing surprise and verifying submodularity.} Surprise is computed in $O(|S|)$ by one pass for the extremes. Verifying the submodular inequality for a pair costs $O(|S|+|T|)$ with hash-set intersection, and certifying submodularity across a family of $n$ setups costs $O(n^2 m)$ with $m$ bounding setup size.

\paragraph{The hull-quotient normal form.} Theorem~\ref{thm:reflect} yields a canonical form: refinement-comparable setups are humor-equivalent iff their hulls agree. Reducing $S$ to $(\min S,\max S)$ is an $O(|S|)$ normalisation under which the repaired universality criterion (Theorem~\ref{thm:repair}) becomes an $O(1)$ interval comparison.

\paragraph{Greedy selection of a comic set.} Because $S \mapsto H(S)$ is monotone and submodular, ``choose $k$ readings maximising surprise'' is monotone submodular maximisation under a cardinality constraint, where greedy attains the classical $1-1/e$ ratio. In one dimension greedy is in fact exact (it selects the two extremes first), but the observation matters for the metric generalisation, where diameters in high-dimensional reading spaces are not determined by two coordinates.

\paragraph{Covariance and monovariance auditing.} $\Cov$ is computed in $O(n)$. Checking monovariance naively costs $O(n^2)$; sorting by humor and verifying that ratings are non-decreasing within ties reduces this to $O(n\log n)$. When monovariance fails, Theorem~\ref{thm:corr} does not apply and the sign of the covariance must be measured, not asserted.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the theory delivers}

\emph{The measurement is forced.} Position blindness, staged additivity and monotonicity determine the invariant up to a unit (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq}).

\emph{Fusion carries a discount.} The exact combination law is submodularity, not merely subadditivity (Theorem~\ref{thm:submod}); shared material is counted once, and the deficit is at least the surprise of the overlap.

\emph{The punchline is the construction that cannot fail.} Coproducts exist unconditionally; products fail exactly when frames are disjoint.

\emph{Universality is a certificate, not a characterisation, until the resolution is fixed.} Terminality maximises every real-valued functor; the converse fails and is repaired exactly by the hull quotient.

\subsection{Limitations}

Readings are points on a line with no internal structure; the invariant survives the metric generalisation, but the combinatorics of Sections 3--5 uses the lattice of finite subsets. Surprise ignores everything but the two extremes --- a feature for uniqueness, a bug for discrimination, and no invariant satisfying (A1)--(A3) can do better. Timing, delivery, taboo and social context sit outside the model. The correlation results concern synthetic data and make no empirical claim about human beings.

\subsection{Relation to incongruity theory}

The classical incongruity--resolution account is recovered thus: \emph{incongruity} is the spread of the reading set, \emph{resolution} is passage to the joint reading (the colimit), and failure of a shared reading to exist is the extreme case in which no resolution within the original frames is possible. The theory adds an exact combination law, a uniqueness theorem for the measurement, and a precise limit/colimit distinction.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{A Wundt threshold for submodular valuations}

Numerical exploration of synthetic datasets displays a regime change: below a surprise threshold, humor and rating correlate at roughly $+0.97$; above it, at roughly $-0.97$; pooled, the correlation collapses to about $+0.04$. This reproduces the inverted-U (``Wundt curve'') of arousal psychology and suggests it is not a psychological accident. The conjecture is that the threshold is the point at which the submodular valuation stops being supermodular on the diagonal: a rating functional $R$ that is a concave increasing transform of a submodular valuation must monovary with the valuation on a down-set and antivary on its complement, with the crossover the unique maximiser of $R$. If true, the inverted-U is a theorem about concavity rather than a fact about people, and the location of the peak is computable from the valuation.

\subsection{Other directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Higher-dimensional reading spaces.} Redevelop the combinatorics with readings in a normed space, where diameter is not determined by two coordinates and greedy submodular maximisation becomes non-trivial.
\item \textbf{Refined invariants.} Seek an invariant that distinguishes interior readings --- necessarily violating one of (A1)--(A3), most plausibly staged additivity --- and determine what universality statement it supports.
\item \textbf{Beyond binary fusion.} Extend the colimit/limit dichotomy to arbitrary diagrams: which diagram shapes admit limits, and does the shape predict comic form (callbacks, running gags, shaggy-dog stories)?
\item \textbf{Timing as a filtration.} Model delivery as a time-indexed filtration of setups; the staged-telling axiom is already an additivity statement across time.
\item \textbf{Empirical programme.} Test monovariance on human rating data stratified by surprise regime, using the paraphrase bound to bound measurement noise from wording variation.
\end{itemize}

